// C implementation limits (limits.h)
//
// This module is signature-only: real where a limit's value is the same
// on every platform Fun targets, prose-only (resolved through
// `c_builtin_owner`) where it genuinely isn't.
//
// Import:
// - `use std.c.limits;`

// Bits in a byte. The same 8 on every platform Fun targets (`CHAR_BIT >
// 8` exists only on now-obscure historical hardware none of them are).
pub const num CHAR_BIT = 8;

// Every signed/unsigned integer range below - `SCHAR_MIN`/`MAX` through
// `ULLONG_MAX`, plus `SIZE_MAX` and `PTRDIFF_MIN`/`MAX` - genuinely
// varies by platform: `long`, notably, is 32-bit on Windows (LLP64) but
// 64-bit on Unix (LP64), so a single Fun-level value would be wrong on
// one of them. `MB_LEN_MAX` (max bytes in a multibyte character) varies
// by locale and platform too. None of these get a real declaration;
// `use std.c.limits;` still makes the name available, resolved through
// `c_builtin_owner`, and whether `char` is signed (which `CHAR_MIN`/
// `CHAR_MAX` depend on) is itself platform-specific.
